<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Montenegro</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Crna Gora</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Montenegro is a country in Southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> on the Balkan Peninsula, with a coastline on the Adriatic Sea. The name, from a Venetian rendering of Crna Gora, &quot;Black Mountain&quot;, came into use in the late 15th century. After Ottoman rule it gained semi-autonomy in 1696 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-petrovic-njegos">House of Petrović-Njegoš</a>, and its independence was recognised by the Great Powers at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> in 1878. It became a kingdom in 1910, then part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and later formed a union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. A referendum on 21 May 2006 passed with 55.5% in favour, and Montenegro declared independence in June 2006.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the collapse of the Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia, Serbia, <b>Montenegro</b> and the Serb rebel-held territories in Croatia agreed on 26 December 1991 to form a new “third Yugoslavia”.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By the beginning of the 1880s Serbia and <b>Montenegro</b> were the only European nations without railroads; one conservative deputy warned that building them would make Serbia suffer the fate of the Indians after the discovery of America.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom united some twelve million people in 1921, rising to about sixteen million by 1941, from regions with very different pasts: former Habsburg Slovenia, Croatia, Vojvodina and Bosnia-Herzegovina, and the formerly Ottoman lands of Serbia, <b>Montenegro</b>, Kosovo and Macedonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The six republics, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Croatia, Macedonia, <b>Montenegro</b>, Serbia and Slovenia, were constitutionally equal; Serbia alone contained two autonomous provinces, Kosovo and Vojvodina, which the 1974 constitution gave voting power equal to the republics', to the lasting anger of Serbian officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Montenegro</b> the Orthodox bishops of Cetinje ruled as prince-bishops from 1516 to 1852.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>…in 1991 and 1992 it remained the anthem of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia, formed by Serbia and <b>Montenegro</b>, and of the State Union of Serbia and Montenegro from 2003, since the two could not agree on new symbols; many Serbs disliked it and booed it at football…</blockquote><a href="/songs/hey-slavs">Hey, Slavs</a></li></ul></section>
